      "construct": "extended-class-with-lookaround",
      "since_pcre2": "10.48",
      "question": "Can an extended class appear in the same pattern as a lookaround?",
      "command": [
        "rg",
        "-oP",
        "(?[\\d-[1]]).(?<!x)",
        "docs/eclass.txt"
      ],
      "expect": "0a",
      "control": [
        "rg",
        "-oP",
        "(?[\\d && ]).(?<!x)",
        "docs/eclass.txt"
      ],
      "control_expect": "error",
      "note": "The one construct whose behaviour actually moved between 10.47 and 10.48. On 10.47 this pattern did not merely fail to match -- it failed to COMPILE, with internal error 170 from check_lookbehinds(), because the extended-class meta codes were absent from that function's switch. Fixed by upstream PRs #961 and #962. The control is a malformed extended class that must still be rejected, which is what makes the first half's success mean 'this composes now' rather than 'the parser waves extended classes through'."
    },
    {
      "id": "P036",
      "tool": "rg",
      "topic": "unicode-and-encodings",
      "construct": "unicode-17-script",
      "since_pcre2": "10.48",
      "question": "Do the Unicode 17.0 scripts resolve, and does the engine still reject junk?",
      "command": [
        "rg",
        "-oP",
        "\\p{Sidetic}",
        "docs/unicode17.txt"
      ],
      "expect": "match",
      "control": [
        "rg",
        "-P",
        "\\p{Not_A_Real_Script_Xyz}",
        "docs/unicode17.txt"
      ],
      "control_expect": "error",
      "note": "PCRE2 validates property names against its compiled Unicode tables, so a script added in Unicode 17.0 is an unknown-property compile error on 10.47 and a valid property at this baseline. The control is load-bearing: it proves the engine rejects invented property names, so acceptance of Sidetic means the tables really were rebuilt rather than that the parser is permissive. Unicode 17.0 added exactly four scripts -- Sidetic, Tai_Yo, Tolong_Siki, Beria_Erfe -- any of which works here."
    }
